    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L05-ACTIVITY-005",
      "SourceSection": "5.2: Machine Depreciation",
      "SourceExcerpt": "After purchase, the value of a machine depreciates exponentially. The table shows its value as a function of years since purchase. If a spreadsheet tool is available, consider using it to help you reason about the following questions.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L05-ACTIVITY-006",
      "SourceSection": "5.2: Machine Depreciation",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The value of the machine in dollars is a function \\(f\\) of time \\(t\\), the number of years since the machine was purchased. Find an equation defining \\(f\\) and be prepared to explain your reasoning.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L05-ACTIVITY-007",
      "SourceSection": "5.2: Machine Depreciation",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Find the value of the machine when \\(t\\) is 0.5 and 1.5. Record the values in the table.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L05-ACTIVITY-008",
      "SourceSection": "5.2: Machine Depreciation",
      "SourceExcerpt": "every one year, say from 1 year to 2 years, or from 0.5 years to 1.5 years?",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L05-ACTIVITY-009",
      "SourceSection": "5.2: Machine Depreciation",
      "SourceExcerpt": "every half a year, say from 0 to 0.5 year, or from 1.5 years to 2 years?",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L05-ACTIVITY-010",
      "SourceSection": "5.2: Machine Depreciation",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Observe the values in the table. By what factor did the value of the machine change:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L05-ACTIVITY-011",
      "SourceSection": "5.2: Machine Depreciation",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Suppose we know \\(f(q)\\), the value of the machine \\(q\\) years since purchase. Explain how we could use \\(f(q)\\) to find \\(f(q+0.5)\\), the value of the machine half a year after that point.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L05-ACTIVITY-012",
      "SourceSection": "5.2: Machine Depreciation",
      "SourceExcerpt": "A bank account is growing exponentially. At the beginning of 2010, the balance, in dollars, was 1,200. At the beginning of 2015, the balance was 1,350. What would the bank account balance be at the beginning of 2018? Give an approximate…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L05-ACTIVITY-013",
      "SourceSection": "5.3: Fever Medicine",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The graph shows the amount of medicine in a child’s body \\(h\\) hours after taking the medicine. The amount of medicine decays exponentially.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L05-ACTIVITY-014",
      "SourceSection": "5.3: Fever Medicine",
      "SourceExcerpt": "After \\(\\frac{1}{4}\\) hour there are about 7 mg of medicine left. After \\(\\frac{3}{4}\\) hour there are about 3.5 mg of medicine left. About how many mg of medicine are left after 1\\(\\frac{3}{4}\\) hours? Explain how you know.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L05-ACTIVITY-015",
      "SourceSection": "5.3: Fever Medicine",
      "SourceExcerpt": "How does the decay rate from \\(\\frac{1}{4}\\) hour to \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) hour compare to the decay rate from \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) hour to \\(\\frac{3}{4}\\) hour? Explain how you know.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L05-ACTIVITY-016",
      "SourceSection": "5.3: Fever Medicine",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Earlier we learned that, for an exponential function, every time the input increases by a certain amount the output changes by a certain factor.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L05-ACTIVITY-017",
      "SourceSection": "5.3: Fever Medicine",
      "SourceExcerpt": "For example, the population of a country, in millions, can be modeled by the exponential function \\(f(c) = 5 \\boldcdot 16^c\\), where \\(c\\) is time in centuries since 1900. By this model, the growth factor for any one century after the…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L05-ACTIVITY-018",
      "SourceSection": "5.3: Fever Medicine",
      "SourceExcerpt": "What about the growth factor for any one decade (one tenth of a century)? Let’s start by finding the growth factors between 1910 and 1920 (\\(c\\) between 0.1 and 0.2) and between 1960 and 1970 (\\(c\\) between 0.6 and 0.7). To do that we can…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L05-ACTIVITY-019",
      "SourceSection": "5.3: Fever Medicine",
      "SourceExcerpt": "from 1910 to 1920: \\(\\dfrac {f(0.2)}{f(0.1)}= \\dfrac {5 \\boldcdot 16^{(0.2)}}{5 \\boldcdot 16^{(0.1)}}\\), which equals \\(16^{(0.1)}\\) (or \\(\\sqrt [10]{16}\\))",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L05-ACTIVITY-020",
      "SourceSection": "5.3: Fever Medicine",
      "SourceExcerpt": "from 1960 to 1970: \\(\\dfrac {f(0.7)}{f(0.6)} = \\dfrac {5 \\boldcdot 16^{(0.7)}}{5 \\boldcdot 16^{(0.6)}}\\), which equals \\(16^{(0.1)}\\) (or \\(\\sqrt [10]{16}\\))",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L05-ACTIVITY-021",
      "SourceSection": "5.3: Fever Medicine",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Now we can generalize about the growth factor for any one decade using the population \\(x\\) centuries after 1900, \\(f(x)\\), and the population one decade (one tenth of a century) after that point, \\(f(x + 0.1)\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L05-ACTIVITY-022",
      "SourceSection": "5.3: Fever Medicine",
      "SourceExcerpt": "from \\(x\\) to \\((x + 0.1)\\): \\(\\dfrac {f(x + 0.1)}{f(x)} = \\dfrac {5 \\boldcdot 16^{(x+0.1)}}{5 \\boldcdot 16^{x}}\\), which also equals \\(16^{(0.1)}\\) (or \\(\\sqrt [10]{16}\\))",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L05-ACTIVITY-023",
      "SourceSection": "5.3: Fever Medicine",
      "SourceExcerpt": "This is consistent with what we know about how exponential functions change over whole-number intervals: they always increase or decrease by equal factors over equal intervals. This is true even when the intervals are fractional.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l05.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
